 "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 3, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.3", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Identify and describe rules and obligations though a wider range of language and expressions in both work and social contexts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": " Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. ", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "The Simple Present Passive", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use an active structure when you want the 'doer' of the action to be the subject. <br /> You use a passive structure when you want the 'receiver' of the action to be the subject.  ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Computer viruses are considered to be one of the major problems in our century. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": " Most of these businesses are linked to the Internet.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Suspects accused of computer crimes are investigated and put on trial.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Active sentence = People (subject) <span class=\"strong\">consider</span> murder (object) to be the most serious crime. <br /><br /> Passive sentence = Murder (subject) is <span class=\"strong\">considered </span>to be the most serious crime (by people). <br /> It is not necessary to mention the object in a passive sentence.  <br /><br />  <br /> You make the passive form of a verb by using tenses of the be verb followed by the past participle.  <br /><br /> The Simple Present passive form is: <br /><br /> am/are/is + past participle <br /><br /> For example:<br /> <span class=\"italic\">English is <span class=\"strong\">spoken</span> in the United States.</span>", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "The Past Perfect Tense", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the Past Perfect tense to identify and talk about an 'earlier past'.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "They can erase all the files that people had saved on a hard drive, or make files useless by changing them.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "One survey found viruses roaming the Internet had infected 98% of North American businesses.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "For those who had intentionally caused damage by transmitting viruses, the punishment could be a stiff prison term plus a fine.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "When you are talking about a past event, you can refer to something that happened at an earlier time to that past event by using the Past Perfect tense.<br /><br />  The structure of the Past Perfect is:<br /><br />  Subject + had + past participle<br /><br /><br />   <span class=\"italic\">When I arrived at the party, Vanessa <span class=\"strong\">had </span>already <span class=\"strong\">gone </span>home. <br /></span> Which event happened <span class=\"italic\">first</span>? Vanessa went home.<br /> Which event happened <span class=\"italic\">later</span>? I arrived at the party.<br /><br />  You use the Past Perfect tense (not Simple Past) when you want to talk about how long something had continued<span class=\"strong\"> up to a past moment.</span> <br /><br />  <span class=\"italic\">Until recently, detectives <span class=\"strong\">had used</span> a limited number of tools to help them solve crimes.<br /> </span> Detectives used a limited number of tools from the past until recently. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to invade", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to enter a location without permission", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A computer virus is a computer program that invades a computer and often causes unwanted things to happen.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about computer viruses.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "undesirable ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "unwanted", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A computer virus is extremely undesirable.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "successive", "type": "name"}, {"content": "following one another", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Each successive generation of computer viruses is getting harder to destroy.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to detect ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to find", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Computer viruses are getting harder and harder to detect.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to hijack", "type": "name"}, {"content": " to take control of something by force", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Viruses can hijack your computer so it can be used for illegal purposes.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "century ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a period of one hundred years", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Computer viruses are considered to be one of the major problems in our century. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "intentionally", "type": "name"}, {"content": " purposefully", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adverb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: For those who had intentionally caused damage by transmitting viruses, the punishment is very serious.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud07", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "stiff", "type": "name"}, {"content": "severe or strong", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There are stiff punishments for people who create computer viruses. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud08", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "fine", "type": "name"}, {"content": "an amount of money to pay as punishment", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The punishment could be a stiff prison term plus a fine.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02aud09", "type": "audio"}]}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob02re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Use the Internet to research a recent computer virus that affected a lot of people. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Summarize the information in your own words.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a paragraph of 100-150 words.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Tell a partner what you learned.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I talk about rules and regulations.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about crime and punishment.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about things I had done or things that had happened.", "type": "text"}]}]}